<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-fiber-product" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Pullback/Fiber product</title>
  <p>
    In this section we describe the <em>pullback</em> (also called <em>fiber product</em>) construction for continuous functions on topological spaces.
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-pullback-top-spaces">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>X,Y</m> and <m>Z</m> be topological spaces. Suppose that <m>f\colon X\to Z</m> and <m>g\colon Y\to Z</m> be continuous maps. The <em>pullback</em> of continuous functions <m>f,g</m> is a topological space <m>X\times_ZY</m> together with continuous functions <m>\pi_X\colon X\times_ZY\to X</m> and <m>\pi_Y\colon X\times_ZY\to Y</m> satisfying the following <em>universal property</em>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <shortdescription>Universal property of pullback</shortdescription>
          <image width="40%">
          <latex-image>
            
\begin{tikzcd}
W \arrow[rdd, bend right,"p_Y"] \arrow[rrd, bend left,"p_X"] \arrow[rd, dotted,"h"] \amp                                \amp             \\
                                                                    \amp X\times_ZY \arrow[d,"\pi_X"] \arrow[r,"\pi_Y"] \amp Y \arrow[d,"g"] \\
                                                                    \amp X \arrow[r,"f"]                    \amp Z          
\end{tikzcd}
          </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
    </statement>
  </definition>
</section>